    {
        "content": "<p>Well, if you try <code>public section</code> and <code>@[expose] def</code>, you'll see the same problem. The problem is that we want exposed definitions to typecheck for every importer but <code>Equiv.refl _</code> will only typecheck with <code>import all Mathlib.Data.List.Sort</code>. Since you <code>@[expose]</code>d <code>test</code>, you can thus not use this defeq (you can use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=finCongr#doc\">docs#finCongr</a> and hide the equality in a <code>by</code> proof though)</p>",
        "id": 578128279,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1773054584
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In order to see that <code>List.insertionSort r []</code> is definitionally equal to <code>[]</code>, you have to unfold <code>List.insertionSort</code>. Because <code>List.insertionSort</code> is not exposed, this would normally not be possible. Since you have <code>import all Mathlib.Data.List.Sort</code>, this makes it possible to unfold <code>List.insertionSort</code>, but only in the private scope. So when you have <code>Equiv.refl _</code> in the definition of <code>test</code>, this only typechecks if you unfold <code>List.insertionSort</code>, which can only happen if you're in the private scope. But <code>@[expose]</code>ing the definition puts the body into the public scope, so it breaks when you add <code>@[expose]</code>.</p>",
        "id": 578128470,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1773054635
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok, many thanks both. This helps and I think solves my problem here.</p>",
